WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.470
- This is Pamela Rae Schuller.

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.790
She's a stand-up comedian,

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.100
and we're about to watch stand-up
that's medically relevant

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.390
and she's gonna explain it to you

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.680
and I'm gonna explain the jokes.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.643
Let's get started.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.460
- I am fully medicated now.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.370
Anybody else, antidepressants, medication?

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.830
- I don't know how I feel
when comedians make that joke.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:17.663
- Why?

00:00:17.663 --> 00:00:20.940
- You shouldn't ever be upset
that you're taking a medicine,

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.737
but celebrating that you're taking it?

00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:24.150
- No, it normalizes it.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.537
How many people are gonna
see this and be like,

00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:27.210
"Oh my gosh, I don't need to be ashamed"?

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.420
- Yes, but then how many people
are gonna request medicines

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.440
that they don't yet need?

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.270
- And then it's up to people like you.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.800
- That's true, that's a lot
of weight on my shoulders.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.633
- Doctors.

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.040
- Well no, it points to the fine line

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.020
between breaking the stigma

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.430
and not spreading misinformation.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.830
- Or celebrating who she is
and what she's going through.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.337
- I'm not anything against celebration.

00:00:45.337 --> 00:00:47.550
- I thought I was on an antidepressant.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:48.690
It turns out I'm not.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.150
It turns out I'm actually
on a mood stabilizer

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.340
that they use as an antidepressant.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.350
Fun fact about prescription drugs,

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.640
everything they prescribe,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.770
they actually use for
four different things.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.603
- That's true,

00:00:59.603 --> 00:01:01.110
and not only do we use it
for four different things,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.440
some don't even have an FDA
approval for those things,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.970
like they haven't been adequately studied,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.830
so we use them off label.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:08.663
- I know that,

00:01:08.663 --> 00:01:10.440
because I've been on so
many meds for Tourettes

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.310
that are not for Tourettes

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.680
because a study showed that
it might help Tourettes.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.570
When I was 12, I wore the nicotine patch

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.680
'cause there was a study.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.640
- Did it help?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.590
- No, it just made me jones.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.080
- Okay, well we had to try something.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.430
You're out of control otherwise.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.760
- So you don't really
know what your deal is

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.100
until you find a combo that works

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.680
and then Google all
your pills by yourself.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.720
So after years of trial and error,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.910
I finally found a combination
of things that worked for me.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.620
- I don't want people
to try it themselves.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.453
- Yeah.

00:01:38.453 --> 00:01:39.960
- Because a lot of these medications

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.480
have really bad interactions,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.670
so you have to have a thorough knowledge

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.380
of how they interact with each other.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.100
If a patient of mine is on
three psychoactive medications,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.750
I get them a psychiatrist ASAP.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.620
- Okay, that's smart.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.390
- Because there's so many interactions

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.130
that I'm not well versed
enough to know how,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.690
to have the experience to
know how they interact.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.523
- That's important.

00:01:58.523 --> 00:02:00.330
- And six months ago,
I decided to Google it,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.163
'cause what the hell?

00:02:01.163 --> 00:02:03.480
And it turns out that
everything I'm taking

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.200
is primarily used for bipolar disorder.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.967
So I went back to my
psychiatrist and I was like,

00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:10.800
"Hey.

00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:15.117
"Do we think?"

00:02:16.506 --> 00:02:18.120
- We.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:18.953
At least it's team based.

00:02:18.953 --> 00:02:21.477
- And she was like, "Oh, yeah."

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.170
- They never had that discussion before?

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.370
- That's terrifying to me.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.650
How many things do you think
my therapist thinks I have

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.790
and just hasn't told me?

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.560
- But why would they not tell you?

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.460
Do you ask?

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:33.750
- All the time, she'll
write something down

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.460
and I'll be like, "What are you writing?"

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.660
- But see, I hate labels,
because the labels aren't really

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.130
for the benefit of the patient.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.800
They're for the insurance
company, the legal stuff.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.500
But really, a person is not a diagnosis.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.420
- So I agree with you 100%.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.460
I think so often, especially
when we work with kids,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.777
we're always trying to find a diagnosis

00:02:51.777 --> 00:02:54.750
instead of getting to know
someone for who they are

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.510
and treating symptoms
of things we're seeing.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.880
But I do think sometimes
knowledge is power.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.070
- Do you think it could become
a self fulfilling prophecy

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.650
where you think, "I am X
so I must behave as X"?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.610
- Yes.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.597
- See, that's also.

00:03:06.597 --> 00:03:08.100
- Yeah, but I, yeah, yeah.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.450
- So it's individualized.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.350
- It's a balancing act.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.660
- I said, "You really didn't
know that I was bipolar?"

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:13.777
And she goes, "No, of course not.

00:03:13.777 --> 00:03:15.960
"We thought we were treating
anxiety and depression."

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:16.793
And I said,

00:03:16.793 --> 00:03:18.427
"Okay, 'cause this kinda feels like

00:03:18.427 --> 00:03:21.537
"a putting your dog's
medication in cheese situation."

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.067
When I was in high school, I was like,

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:25.440
"Dad, I think I'm depressed."

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.127
And he was like, "You
just need some protein.

00:03:27.127 --> 00:03:28.320
"Get a scoop of peanut butter."

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.200
- That's every health
influencer on TikTok.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.100
Health influencer.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.003
- I think, yeah.

00:03:33.003 --> 00:03:34.657
- "Buy my protein!"

00:03:34.657 --> 00:03:36.510
"Buy my hormone!"

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:37.343
You think I'm making that up?

00:03:37.343 --> 00:03:38.940
- Mine would be amazing.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:39.773
- Yeah?

00:03:39.773 --> 00:03:41.070
- Do you wanna buy some
of my hormones, Mike?

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:41.903
- Yes.

00:03:41.903 --> 00:03:43.810
But yours aren't hormones,
they're horny moans.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.403
- No.

00:03:46.403 --> 00:03:47.572
- Stand-up comedy, ay.

00:03:47.572 --> 00:03:50.032
- No, you're canceled for that.

00:03:50.032 --> 00:03:51.450
There's a reason you're a doctor.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.740
- Yeah, who makes jokes.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.030
- Yeah, there's a lot of dads out there

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.060
just white-knuckling it
through life right now,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.200
clinging to a jar of chunky
Jif like it's the answer.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.727
Just a buoy in the storm.

00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:05.370
"It'll pass."

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.800
- That's a joke with a lot
of medical meaning behind it.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:08.633
- Tell me more.

00:04:08.633 --> 00:04:12.480
- Men will oftentimes
experience their depression

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.340
or show their depression to the world

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.890
in different ways than what
we describe traditionally

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:17.910
in medical stuff.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.520
So for example, some
will develop addiction

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.400
to going to the gym, to taking
steroids and getting huge.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.870
Some will get a gambling addiction.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.880
Some will become abusive
in their relationship.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.980
But really, what they're
experiencing is depression

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.420
and they're acting out.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.160
- Look at me, learning.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.530
And that was interesting,
so I was actively listening.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.490
- Yeah, that was good.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:36.540
I don't know if you were faking it,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.373
you're a really good actress.

00:04:37.373 --> 00:04:39.583
- No, you know when I'm faking it.

00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:40.416
- Okay.

00:04:40.416 --> 00:04:42.330
- My doctor comes in.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.760
He doesn't say hello, he
doesn't say good morning.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.960
He just walks in.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.390
He points at me, which
