- He's a (censored) idiot.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.133
He's not even an MD.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.980
He's a goddamn DO!

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.080
- Why is he so angry about that?

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.100
I never understood this joke.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.151
- He burned you through the computer!

00:09:55.151 --> 00:09:56.400
- Yeah but why?

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.050
- So this dude is all
up in my merchandise.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.970
He's like, "Aw bro, I know what's wrong."

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:01.680
I'm like, "Stop saying bro."

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.903
He's like, "Son, oh.

00:10:06.547 --> 00:10:07.747
"You got too much blood down there.

00:10:07.747 --> 00:10:08.887
"It's lowering your sperm count.

00:10:08.887 --> 00:10:10.361
"So we're gonna do a dangerous surgery

00:10:10.361 --> 00:10:12.360
"called varicose seal repair."

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.380
- A varicose seal is basically
a swelling of your veins

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:17.213
in your scrotum.

00:10:17.213 --> 00:10:19.350
We used to say, if you feel that area,

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.180
it feels like a bag of worms.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.519
Can you think of a more poop explanation?

00:10:23.519 --> 00:10:25.620
- Thank you so much for that visual.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.120
- That's what they taught
us in medical school.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.357
- This dude pulls out
a trimmer and he goes,

00:10:28.357 --> 00:10:29.903
"Let me shave you down right now.

00:10:31.088 --> 00:10:32.160
"Let's do this right here, right now."

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.387
I go, "Arjam, back up.

00:10:34.387 --> 00:10:35.427
"I'm not ready."

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:40.983
He goes, "Listen, man, if
you don't get this surgery,

00:10:42.487 --> 00:10:46.017
"you can't have kids ever."

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:49.533
Are you serious?

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.653
Life gets very real when
don't want becomes can't have.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:58.860
Isn't that right, DOs?

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.800
- He's so anti-DO, which is
funny because in this clip,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.950
the DO ends up allowing him to conceive.

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.909
- Yeah, to be clear,
I'm not at all anti-DO.

00:11:06.909 --> 00:11:09.870
- I don't think he's anti-DO either.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.130
I think he's just making a joke.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:11.963
- [Pamela] Yes.

00:11:11.963 --> 00:11:14.220
- But it's just at the cost of doctors

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.690
in the midst of a pandemic,

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.190
when doctors were on the front lines.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.060
- That's totally fair.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.090
- Terrible!

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.140
Look, as a comedian, you
gotta know comedic timing.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:22.973
- Yes.

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:24.330
- And if you're gonna
make a joke about doctors.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:25.614
- When was this filmed?

00:11:25.614 --> 00:11:26.839
- Peak pandemic.

00:11:26.839 --> 00:11:27.672
- [Pamela] Okay.

00:11:27.672 --> 00:11:30.540
- My dermatologist said
he needed to do a biopsy

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.180
of a little mole on the tip of my nose.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:34.050
- I was about to say,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:35.850
you don't need a
dermatologist in some cases.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:38.070
I can do that, but not if it's on the face

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:39.870
'cause I'm worried about
scarring and stuff.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:42.360
- Wow, that sounds like it's gonna hurt.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.407
He said, "Don't worry,
we're gonna numb it first

00:11:44.407 --> 00:11:45.657
"with a painkiller."

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:47.580
I said, "How?"

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.657
He said, "We're gonna
puncture a sharp needle

00:11:49.657 --> 00:11:51.720
"into the tip of your nose."

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.860
- When I work with patients

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.870
and they need a small procedure done,

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.760
sometimes I say I think it might be best

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:57.780
to go without anesthesia,

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.720
because the anesthesia might
hurt more than the procedure.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.730
- I had to get a little thing removed

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.310
and she did the anesthesia,
she gave the shot

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:06.750
and then she started slicing

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.940
and I started immediately
crying and yelped.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:09.867
- You have to wait.

00:12:09.867 --> 00:12:12.270
- And she goes, "Well it
takes 15 minutes to kick in."

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:15.204
And I was like, "Then wait 15 minutes!"

00:12:15.204 --> 00:12:16.468
She was annoyed that she had to.

00:12:16.468 --> 00:12:17.301
- When I did my derm rotation,

00:12:17.301 --> 00:12:18.930
you would inject patients with lidocaine,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:21.210
move on to the next patient,
figure out what's going on,

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.940
go back to that patient, do the procedure.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:24.773
- Uh huh.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.787
- And at the hospital, I said,

00:12:27.787 --> 00:12:30.326
"Excuse me, can I have pain medication

00:12:30.326 --> 00:12:32.817
"'cause I am not thriving?"

00:12:32.817 --> 00:12:35.526
And the nurse said, "Oh
okay", and he gives me the IV

00:12:35.526 --> 00:12:36.840
and he gives me a little
pump of something,

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.750
and I was like, "Yum yum
yum yum, yes she's alive!"

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.407
And then I was like, "Actually
no, I still passed away.

00:12:41.407 --> 00:12:43.230
"What the (censored) was that?"

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:46.740
And he said it was a placebo,
it was a saline injection.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:49.687
And I said, "Sir, my ankle
looks like a (censored) U-turn.

00:12:49.687 --> 00:12:51.490
"You better give me some
(censored) (censored)."

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.090
And I don't know if you guys know this,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.490
but black and brown people
are prescribed opioids

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.710
at a lower rate than white people,

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.680
so white people have a higher
addiction rate to heroin.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.447
And I said to myself, "Huh,
maybe sometimes racism's good?"

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.420
- I will say, it's a double edged sword.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.510
Let's say you're in a community
where I worked in Newark.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:14.070
You had patients that'd come in

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.490
that you know are at high risk
because of their environment

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.440
for developing an opioid addiction.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.020
If you're a good doctor, you should know

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.750
that if you live in a
white suburban community,

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.322
you're also at a high risk.

00:13:26.322 --> 00:13:27.155
- Okay, good, yeah.

00:13:27.155 --> 00:13:29.010
- But being aware of someone's risks

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.830
and tailoring their medication
plan is actually good.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.260
So in this case, the
doctor that's assuming

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.850
that because someone's white,

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.910
they have a lower risk
is factually inaccurate,

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.680
and therefore has led to worse outcomes.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.447
So it's when biases work against you,

00:13:42.447 --> 00:13:43.977
not when racism works for you.

00:13:43.977 --> 00:13:44.970
You get what I'm saying?

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:45.803
- I do.

00:13:45.803 --> 00:13:46.800
Before major surgery,

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:48.660
I asked if I could get
something to take the edge off.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:49.493
I already had an IV in and I was like,

00:13:49.493 --> 00:13:51.330
"Can you give me a little
something something?"

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.060
We're running late, I was
freaking out and they said no.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:57.300
And then I started
barking and then I got it.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:00.540
Then it went from a scary
day to such a fun day.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.740
I was telling jokes.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.060
I did stand-up for everyone.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:04.440
- I would've rather you called me

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.090
and I would've just held your hand.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:06.923
- That would not have helped.

00:14:06.923 --> 00:14:08.580
- Some people will say I'm a medication.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:09.870
- Okay, they're lying.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.060
- I started walking in my sleep.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.890
I was living with my
girlfriend at the time

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.194
and I started having a recurring dream

00:14:16.194 --> 00:14:21.194
that there was a hovering
insect-like jackal in our bedroom.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.743
- I had the same dream, but it
wasn't a jackal, it was you.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:27.692
- That's because I was there.

00:14:27.692 --> 00:14:29.059
- Oh.

00:14:29.059 --> 00:14:29.892
- I was just like.

00:14:29.892 --> 00:14:32.610
- I did buy a book, it was
called "The Promise of Sleep"

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.850
and I learned that there are
78 known sleep disorders,

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:37.920
things that range from sleep apnea

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.690
to night terrors to narcolepsy.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:40.710
Narcolepsy is terrifying

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.440
'cause there are people who
fall asleep at any time.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:45.420
- I think there's more than 78.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.320
- Sleep disorders?

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:47.153
How many do you have?

00:14:47.153 --> 00:14:48.030
- Primarily one.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:49.050
- Insomnia?

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:49.883
- Yeah.

00:14:49.883 --> 00:14:50.716
- Yeah.

00:14:50.716 --> 00:14:51.549
- Yeah, anxiety induced, mostly.

00:14:51.549 --> 00:14:54.270
- And I read about this thing
called REM behavior disorder

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.430
where people have a dopamine deficiency.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.170
That's the chemical that's
released from your brain

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.290
into your body when you fall
asleep that paralyzes your body

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:04.500
so you don't do what's in your brain.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:08.400
- Interesting, I don't
know if that's 100% proven

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:09.810
or the leading theory.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:13.050
Basically, when you fall
asleep and you enter REM stage,

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.910
your muscles are supposed
to become paralyzed

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:16.950
so you don't move during
this stage of your sleep

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:17.783
while you're dreaming.

00:15:17.783 --> 00:15:19.394
- That's why I don't hit REM.

00:15:19.394 --> 00:15:20.227
- Oh.

00:15:20.227 --> 00:15:21.300
- Tourette-ta-Tourette-Tourette-Tourettes.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:22.133
- Really?

00:15:22.133 --> 00:15:23.015
- Yeah.

00:15:23.015 --> 00:15:24.780
- So do you not feel
refreshed when you wake up?

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:25.980
- Not very often, no.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.790
- Five years ago, I'm in
Walla Walla, Washington

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:31.380
and I'm staying, which is a place,

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:35.007
and I'm staying at a hotel
called La Quinte Inn.

00:15:35.007 --> 00:15:40.007
And I fall asleep and I have a dream

00:15:40.174 --> 00:15:45.174
that there is a guided missile
headed towards my motel room

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.670
and there was all this military personnel

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:48.503
in the room with me.

00:15:48.503 --> 00:15:49.890
I jump out of bed.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:54.810
So I decided, in my dream, and
as it turns out, in my life,

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:57.390
to jump out my window.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.890
- Oh no.

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:00.270
- There are two important details.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.610
One, I was on the second floor.

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:05.820
Two, the window was closed.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.757
So I jump through a window
and I screamed "Ahhh!"

00:16:08.757 --> 00:16:11.040
- And it's not like one
of those action movies

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.317
where the window gets blown
up before you jump through it.

00:16:13.317 --> 00:16:14.150
- No, yeah.

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:15.120
- He's literally flying through a window.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.153
- And I got up and I kept running,

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:22.080
and I'm running and I'm slowly realizing

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.560
I'm on the front lawn of La Quinte Inn

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:30.390
in Walla Walla, Washington
in my underwear bleeding,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:32.067
and I'm like, "Oh no".

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.270
- What does it say about him, maybe men,

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:39.510
that it took him literally
jumping out of a window

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:41.823
to be like, "Maybe I should
get some help for this"?

00:16:41.823 --> 00:16:43.623
- Well that's literally
what I was gonna say.

00:16:43.623 --> 00:16:44.550
When people ask me

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:46.980
when is the time that you should
see a doctor for something?

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:47.880
That's the time.

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:49.030
- I would say one day before that.

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:50.940
- Yeah, but it's hard
to predict sometimes.

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:53.010
But that's the definite line.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:54.930
- And the next morning,
I flew back to New York

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.640
and I did what I should've
done in the first place

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.170
when I saw the jackal.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.872
I went to a doctor who
specializes in sleep disorders

00:17:00.872 --> 00:17:03.657
and I was diagnosed with
REM behavior disorder.

00:17:03.657 --> 00:17:06.570
And so now when I go to bed
at night, I take medication

00:17:06.570 --> 00:17:11.263
and I sleep, and I'm not making
this up, in a sleeping bag

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.780
up to my neck and I wear mittens

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:21.660
so I can't open the sleeping bag.

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.100
- There's gotta be a better solution.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.020
- That sounds so comfortable.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:26.132
- No it doesn't.

00:17:26.132 --> 00:17:26.965
- Yes it does.

00:17:26.965 --> 00:17:28.100
- Struggling to escape?

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:29.940
- I love small spaces.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:31.830
Wrap me up like a burrito.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:32.663
- Really?

00:17:32.663 --> 00:17:33.496
- Yeah.

00:17:33.496 --> 00:17:34.620
- With gloves that you're unable to open?

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:35.574
- Yeah.

00:17:35.574 --> 00:17:36.407
The problem is, as a single woman,

00:17:36.407 --> 00:17:38.400
they would then find me six years later.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.590
- That's literally what I do to babies

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.990
when they scratch their faces.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:45.323
- And then when you unzip
babies in the morning, they go.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:48.690
- Like I said, I have Tourette Syndrome.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.100
- The best one!

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.750
When I'm with other people with Tourettes,

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:53.280
I pick up on their tics.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:54.180
That's pretty normal.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.130
So you're about to see
my Tourettes gets worse

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.060
when I watch him.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:57.893
- Oh.

00:17:57.893 --> 00:17:59.253
Camera, watch intently.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.410
- This twitch gets me in trouble.

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:02.760
My roommate came home from work.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:03.660
I was like, "Dude.

00:18:06.127 --> 00:18:07.557
"Our neighbor died."

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.807
He said, "Did you kill him?"

00:18:14.341 --> 00:18:15.537
I was like, "Nah."

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.100
- That's the same as your pick-up joke.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:21.870
- No, it's my customs joke

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.060
where he asked if I'm
bringing anything illegal

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.700
back into the country
and I was like, "No."

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:30.873
- Click here for when Pam
reads thirsty tweets to me.

